@startuml bisection-instrument-floor
' ============================================================================
' THE INSTRUMENT FLOOR (#187).  12,288 B is a property of the ALGORITHM, not of
' any subject, and it is derivable without running anything.
'
' Diagram type: an ACTIVITY DIAGRAM over a annotated INTERVAL LADDER.  Chosen
' because the floor is a control-flow fact — a subject that survives the first
' probe never enters the doubling loop — so the claim is only visible if the
' branch that is NOT taken is drawn.  A bar chart of results would hide it.
'
' NOTE — ACTIVITY-DIAGRAM LEGENDS DO NOT TYPESET LaTeX in this PlantUML build.
' Verified on a minimal case: a <latex> span in an activity diagram's legend emits
' ZERO typeset images and leaks the literal tag. Math therefore lives in the NOTES
' (which do typeset), and this legend is deliberately plain prose. Do not "restore"
' LaTeX to the legend cells.
'
' Colour convention — the report's Appendix E.3 mapping:
'   TEAL   #00695C the gate / the measuring instrument
'   RED    #C62828 the floor: an artefact reported as a measurement
'   GREEN  #2E7D32 a genuine measurement (above the floor)
'   AMBER  #EF6C00 the un-askable case
' ============================================================================
skinparam backgroundColor #FAFAFA
skinparam defaultFontName "DejaVu Sans"
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam ArrowThickness 1.4
skinparam ActivityBackgroundColor #E0F2F1
skinparam ActivityBorderColor #00695C
skinparam ActivityDiamondBackgroundColor #FFF3E0
skinparam ActivityDiamondBorderColor #EF6C00

title <b>12,288 B is an INSTRUMENT FLOOR, not a measurement</b>\nprovable from the algorithm — <i>twelve of fifteen subjects read the SAME floor,\nwhich is ONE artefact, not twelve agreeing measurements</i>

|#E0F2F1|the instrument — `min_stack_for(name, depth)`|
start
:`hi` <latex>\leftarrow</latex> `PROBE_START` = <latex>16 \times 1024 = 16{,}384</latex> B;
note right #E0F2F1
  **`PROBE_START` is the whole of #187.** It fixes the smallest
  answer the function can ever return.
end note

if (does the subject survive at <latex>hi = 16{,}384</latex>?) then (**yes** — the common case)
  :**the doubling loop is NEVER ENTERED**;<<#FFCDD2>>
  :bisect on <latex>[\,8{,}192,\ 16{,}384\,]</latex>;<<#FFCDD2>>
  note right #FFCDD2
    <latex>hi - lo = 8{,}192 > \mathtt{RESOLUTION} = 4{,}096</latex>,
    so the loop runs, and it terminates on its FIRST midpoint:
    <latex>mid = \frac{8{,}192 + 16{,}384}{2} = 12{,}288</latex>
    <latex>\Rightarrow</latex> **12,288 is the smallest value the bisection can emit.**
  end note
  :⚠ **BEFORE #187** — return `12288`\n<i>a bare `usize`, indistinguishable from a\nsubject genuinely measured at 12,288 B</i>;<<#EF9A9A>>
  :★ **AFTER #187** (`125065a8`) — return\n`MinStack::BelowResolution`\n<i>renders `<12 KiB (BELOW THE INSTRUMENT FLOOR)`,\n**never a number**</i>;<<#A5D6A7>>
else (**no**)
  :double: `hi` <latex>\leftarrow</latex> <latex>2\,hi</latex>, up to 512 MiB;
  :bisect on <latex>[\,hi/2,\ hi\,]</latex>\n<latex>\Rightarrow</latex> a GENUINE measurement,\nabove the floor;<<#A5D6A7>>
endif

:⚠★ **the defect neither prior analysis named**\n`runs_within` mapped an `execve` refusal to `Err(_) => false` —\n**the same verdict as a genuine overflow** — so the instrument\ncould not distinguish *"needs more stack"* from *"cannot ask"*;<<#FFE082>>
note right #FFE082
  ★ **Status at HEAD: REPAIRED, and the repair is narrower than the
  diagnosis.** `runs_within` now panics on
  <latex>\mathtt{ErrorKind::NotFound}</latex> (a missing probe binary is
  no longer readable as a fault), and the surviving
  `Err(_) => false` arm carries an explicit justification: at the
  bottom of the exponential probe the rlimit can be too small for the
  kernel to lay out the child's stack at all, and treating that as
  *"did not survive at this bound"* is what keeps the bisection
  **monotone**.
  <latex>\Rightarrow</latex> The conflation is closed for the case that
  mattered; the remaining arm is a deliberate, documented choice.
end note
floating note left #FFCDD2
  ★★ **Why this matters more than a rounding error.** A slope is
  <latex>\dfrac{B(d_{hi}) - B(d_{lo})}{d_{hi} - d_{lo}}</latex>. When **both** ends read
  the floor the slope is exactly <latex>0</latex> — which is *correct* — but the
  two endpoint BYTE figures are not measurements and must not be divided
  by, and a row reading
  <latex>(12{,}288 - 12{,}288)/\mathrm{span} = 0.0</latex> looks exactly
  like a result.
  **Slope readings survive the floor; endpoint values do not.**
end note
stop

legend bottom
  |= cell |= meaning |
  |<#E0F2F1>| the instrument's own control flow |
  |<#FFCDD2>| the floor region — **no subject information reaches the answer** |
  |<#EF9A9A>| the pre-#187 return: an artefact spelled as a measurement |
  |<#A5D6A7>| a value that carries real subject information |
  |<#FFE082>| the un-askable case — *"cannot ask"* is not *"needs more stack"* |
endlegend

@enduml
